      "title": "Altered Dominants and Jazz Colour Chords",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** chords | **Difficulty:** 8/10 | **Time:** ~10 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Altered dominants intensify V7 resolution through sharpened or flattened extensions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "altered-dominants",
      "title": "Altered dominants",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "G7(♭9), G7(♯5) heighten tension before C major/minor. The altered scale (super Locrian) matches these tensions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tritone-substitution",
      "title": "Tritone substitution",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Replace G7 with D♭7 — shared tritone (F and B/C♭) allows similar resolution. Secondary dominants temporarily tonicise other degrees: A7→Dm in C is V7/vi."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "trad-crossover",
      "title": "Trad crossover",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Recognise G7♯9 vs plain G7 in fusion settings. When unsure, play 3rd and 7th clearly."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
